The reading begins with ordinary thermal observations, language, units, measurements and explicit heat/work conventions, then follows closed/open balances, properties, entropy/exergy, potentials and stability, mixtures/reactions, phase coexistence, thermal machines, conduction/transients, convection, radiation, phase-change/coupled transport, exchangers/storage and physical evidence. Later material develops inverse problems, experiments, microscopic carriers/interfaces, ultrafast response, electronics cooling, process heat, control and continuing service. Complete introductory/classical/transport books and public advanced courses support substantial independent exercises. The completion description demands advanced work in each of six subject groups, an integrated service and an independent research/criticism cycle; practical completion additionally requires a performed physical investigation. Those are reader learning demands, not claims that the future laboratory and research work has already been performed.

Whole-book sources include Yan, Cleynen, DeVoe and the Lienhards, with UFJF/UFSC Portuguese companions and MIT/NPTEL programmes. Primary IAPWS, CoolProp, FiPy and NIST methods support property computation, discretization and measurements; complete thermoreflectance and nanoscale research supports the deeper studies. The NASA heat-pipe handbook is dated June 1979 despite its 1981 accession identifier, and the separate loop-heat-pipe course is from 2023. Preserve source versions and access distinctions: the historical UMD DeVoe URLs return 404 while the attributed CC BY 4.0 mirror is available; the Lienhard author PDF's intermittent retrieval does not justify claiming every page was inspected or a general CC licence. MIT 2.43 has one conventional homework assignment plus explanatory quizzes/oral assessment; MIT 2.51 is advanced undergraduate; MIT 2.57's available complete reference notes are from Fall 2004; MIT 2.58J lists lectures 24–26 without attached notes. The September 2026 thermoreflectance review is identified as a versioned preprint. Books, articles, PDFs, course media, data and third-party software remain external links.

All four original programs were extracted from the rendered Records and run in fresh Python 3.13.13 processes from another working directory. Finite-body energy/entropy/work passed 388 cases against independently reduced 70-digit Decimal equations and Newton iteration, with symmetry, equal-temperature and scaling checks. Transient layered conduction passed 60 exact rational matrix cases, two independent steady resistance profiles, forty reversal/scaling cases and four Fourier/continuum refinement cases; maximum errors at 8/16/32/64 cells were about 0.0288254, 0.00726097, 0.00181865 and 0.000454876 K, demonstrating second-order convergence at a fixed physical time. Radiation passed 64 exact two-plane cases, 27 unequal-area enclosure cases and 80 independent successive-reflection calculations, with closure and scaling checks. Counterflow exchange passed 148 high-precision LMTD duty solutions and 24 independent differential-equation shooting comparisons, including equal/nearly-equal capacity rates and limiting cases. One hundred fifty invalid domains were rejected and fourteen additional teaching calculations passed. These bounded examples verify the stated models and numerical behavior; they do not validate arbitrary equipment or an unperformed physical experiment.

