The reading starts from observations, units, fluid properties, pressure/hydrostatics, fields and necessary mathematics, then develops integral/local conservation, constitutive and boundary assumptions, similarity, exact viscous and potential solutions, vorticity/circulation, pipe systems/transients, boundary layers/separation, instability and turbulent descriptions. Compressibility, shocks/nozzles, characteristics, free-surface/open-channel waves, buoyant/rotating/stratified flow, interfaces/wetting/cavitation, particles/multiphase flow, complex-fluid constitutive laws/rheometry, mixing/transport, microfluidic and structural coupling follow. The numerical and measurement progression includes actual experimental response, inverse problems, conservative discretization, pressure coupling, mesh/time verification, physical validation and continuing service. Completion demands full sustained readings, advanced investigations across the main families, performed experimental work, an integrated service and independent research with criticism/revision. These are reader learning demands, not claims of already performed laboratory or research projects.

The sources include complete McDonough, UFPR, Liburdy, Tong, Fitzpatrick, Brennen and CFD texts, MIT graduate material, NPTEL fluid/hydraulic/gas/experimental programmes, Hinch and the MIT rheology course, NIST flow methods, PIV uncertainty and primary numerical/physical evidence. The UFPR text has eight chapters and 208 PDF pages, with no asserted publication year or general adaptation licence. Tong's webpage presents five downloads while the inspected PDFs number six chapters; assignments use the PDF numbering. MIT rheology topics 4 and 6–8 have no attached public notes, so the progression identifies that gap and uses Hinch's fuller notes. The 2024 Hinch file was institutionally indexed but direct automated retrieval failed; its entire contents are not falsely claimed inspected. TMR moved in February 2026, and OpenFOAM Foundation version 14 was released in July 2026. NACA 1135 is the 1953 report. Source Records distinguish open adaptation rights from authorized free reading, metadata from inspected text, and course access from missing media or assessment. Books, articles, films, datasets, meshes and third-party software remain external links.

All four original programs were extracted from the rendered Records, hashed and run in fresh Python 3.13.13 processes from another working directory. Ninety-six pipe operating cases passed against independently constructed 65-digit Decimal/Newton friction and head calculations, with three nearly equal pump/static-head cases checked against the exact supplied binary inputs and an unresolved transitional operating point rejected. Seventy-two nozzle cases passed against a separate high-precision construction using the downstream sonic area, independently inverted exit state, jump conservation and entropy. The Blasius solution was checked against an independent high-order Decimal Taylor integration and Töpfer rescaling; wall-gradient errors at 240/480/960 intervals over eta=12 were approximately 5.54e-10, 3.55e-11 and 2.32e-12, while separate domain refinements exposed truncation error. Thirty exact rational shallow-water updates passed; wet Riemann comparisons at 64/128/256/512 cells had decreasing L1 errors about 0.00905, 0.00609, 0.00412 and 0.00269. Three small-amplitude wave refinements, uniform states, reflection, global mass/momentum and energy checks also passed. Thirty-two invalid-domain cases and nineteen additional teaching calculations passed. The examples establish their stated bounded models and numerical behavior, not arbitrary real equipment performance.

